WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.159 --> 00:00:04.200
So welcome back.

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:07.740
Today, we are in Washington, DC, at the World Bank.

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:13.330
And I am particularly excited because we have, as our guest, Jishnu Das,

00:00:13.330 --> 00:00:16.550
who is an economist at the World Bank.

00:00:16.550 --> 00:00:22.680
And Jishnu has really been at the frontier of thinking about and measuring health care

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:25.320
quality in low and middle income countries.

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:27.550
Jishnu, thank you so much for being here.

00:00:27.550 --> 00:00:28.800
Pleasure to be here, Ashish.

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:30.009
Look forward to it.

00:00:30.009 --> 00:00:32.489
So, Jishnu, let's get right into it.

00:00:32.488 --> 00:00:35.430
There is a lot of interest in thinking about health care

00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:38.220
quality in high income countries.

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:41.530
There's been a lot of work that's been done in the US and England.

00:00:41.530 --> 00:00:44.560
Most of us feel like we know less about the state of health care

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:48.059
quality in low income countries or middle income countries.

00:00:48.059 --> 00:00:51.320
What is the state of health care quality in India?

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:56.149
I think it's fair to say that the state of health quality in India is not good,

00:00:56.149 --> 00:00:59.059
and there are many, many ways you could look at that.

00:00:59.059 --> 00:01:02.460
First of all, the country's bifurcated in two.

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:05.660
There's a better-performing south and a worse-performing north.

00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:10.040
And I think you see that in every held statistic that we have.

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:12.250
The second part of it, which I think is less known,

00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:17.500
is that that bifurcation carries over to health care quality.

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:19.910
And you can think about that in a couple of ways.

00:01:19.910 --> 00:01:22.459
The one way I like to think about health care quality

00:01:22.459 --> 00:01:29.550
is to just try and see what happens when you, as a patient, go to a doctor?

00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:33.640
And then say, how, as researchers, can we mimic that experience?

00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:36.890
How can I tell you a little bit about the average person who goes to a doctor

00:01:36.890 --> 00:01:37.970
and what happens to them?

00:01:37.970 --> 00:01:42.780
I think the simplest way to think about it is to say, pick a condition that's really,

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:43.780
really salient.

00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:48.420
So, for instance, we've picked chest pain in a middle-aged man.

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:52.920
Which usually, in a place like the US, 98% of the time

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:55.360
will be diagnosed correctly.

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:58.080
And in the case we picked, for instance, if the doctor

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:01.200
had questioned appropriately, done the right exams,

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:04.890
he or she would have diagnosed it as unstable angina

00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:08.140
or what's, in common parlance, called a heart attack.

00:02:08.139 --> 00:02:13.020
So we train people to depict these symptoms very, very carefully

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:15.570
and send them to many, many doctors.

00:02:15.570 --> 00:02:18.270
And I think the simplest way you could think about poor health quality

00:02:18.270 --> 00:02:23.630
is you can ask, how many of these patients would receive the right treatment and nothing

00:02:23.630 --> 00:02:24.630
else?

00:02:24.630 --> 00:02:26.550
That answer is about 5%.

00:02:26.550 --> 00:02:31.070
So things got slightly better when you say, how many of these guys

00:02:31.070 --> 00:02:34.300
would have received the right treatment but would have also

00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:35.480
received something else?

00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:37.530
Maybe the doctor did the right thing, but he also

00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:40.080
did a bunch of things that were not totally correct.

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:43.150
So that's about 40 to 45%.

00:02:43.150 --> 00:02:50.560
But you're still left with a majority of doctors not being able to pick up, diagnose, or treat

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:56.830
a very textbook case of chest pain that, not appropriately diagnosed and

00:02:56.830 --> 00:02:59.790
treated, would lead into a heart attack.

00:02:59.790 --> 00:03:04.281
Just so I understand what you're describing, you take standardized patients, people who

00:03:04.281 --> 00:03:07.740
have been trained, and you send them off.

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:11.590
And about 5% of the time, the physician gets it right

00:03:11.590 --> 00:03:16.510
and doesn't do anything else and a majority of the time,

00:03:16.510 --> 00:03:17.920
they don't even get it right at all.

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:19.040
So what's going on?

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:20.250
Where is the failure?

00:03:20.250 --> 00:03:22.710
Are they not asking the right questions?

00:03:22.710 --> 00:03:24.350
Are they not doing the right exam?

00:03:24.350 --> 00:03:27.930
What do we know about why people have a hard time arriving

00:03:27.930 --> 00:03:29.720
at the right diagnosis and treatment?

00:03:29.720 --> 00:03:33.970
The first failure could be that the doctor just doesn't know.

00:03:33.970 --> 00:03:37.720
I mean, the guy hasn't been trained, he doesn't have the medical knowledge,

00:03:37.720 --> 00:03:38.840
perhaps.

00:03:38.840 --> 00:03:42.680
And if he doesn't know that a chest pain could be a heart attack,

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:43.800
maybe that's the biggest problem.

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:47.510
And, in fact, if you read a lot of the literature on health care, a lot of it

00:03:47.510 --> 00:03:52.270
is about the &amp;quot;massive shortage&amp;quot; of health care workers or a staffing

00:03:52.270 --> 00:03:53.270
problem.

00:03:53.270 --> 00:03:55.620
And they don't have sufficiently trained doctors.

00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:59.730
The second part of it, which I think is less salient

00:03:59.730 --> 00:04:04.190
in the kind of health policy world, but hits us, as patients,

00:04:04.190 --> 00:04:10.430
every day is that the doctor may not put in enough effort.

00:04:10.430 --> 00:04:13.550
So I don't know how many people have been to clinics in low income

00:04:13.550 --> 00:04:16.380
countries, but unlike, say, clinics in the US

00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:19.739
where you're cordoned off from the other patients, there's a door,

00:04:19.738 --> 00:04:23.300
you're alone with the doctor, the doctor listens to you.

00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:25.940
A typical clinic that we work in-- if you go in,

00:04:25.940 --> 00:04:28.150
there are five people sitting in the clinic.

00:04:28.150 --> 00:04:31.060
These could be patients, might be other people, might be friends,

00:04:31.060 --> 00:04:32.990
might be people from the community.

00:04:32.990 --> 00:04:37.210
The TV is blaring, the patient comes in, sits, the doctor's talking on his cell

00:04:37.210 --> 00:04:38.210
phone.

00:04:38.210 --> 00:04:39.210
He'll pick up every call.

00:04:39.210 --> 00:04:41.320
So it's an extremely distracted environment.

00:04:41.320 --> 00:04:45.881
There are various things going on, and you want to start worrying about, is the doctor

00:04:45.881 --> 00:04:49.460
putting in enough effort to look after you.

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:55.810
The third issue is, sometimes-- and this has been a worry especially with India

00:04:55.810 --> 00:04:59.780
and other countries-- people think, well, maybe the doctor

00:04:59.780 --> 00:05:04.350
is getting paid to do something which they know is not right.

00:05:04.350 --> 00:05:09.990
One way to think about it in India is, maybe they're getting paid to ask you to get a bunch

00:05:09.990 --> 00:05:12.860
of tests done because they're getting kickbacks from the industry.

